; `for` has four shapes and one body expression, and a form that is
; none of them is a parse error rather than a type error.
;
; `(for i in lo..hi body)`, `(for i in lo..hi by step body)`,
; `(for x in xs body)` and `(for (x k) in xs body)`. The `..` marks a
; range and `by` its step, so after them, and after a container, the
; body is the only element left. That forces the one-body rule, and
; `{ ... }` is how several expressions are sequenced.
;
; So a second body cannot belong to any shape, and the expectation
; says so. `.axbad` because a parse error stops the file, so this case
; carries exactly one malformed form.
(import IO)

(:: main Int)

(fn (main) (for i in 0..5 1 2 3))
